  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory continue through Friday \nevening as dangerous heat and humidity remains in place. Afternoon \ntemperatures will soar into the mid to upper 90s to around 100 \nacross most of the area each day. Oppressive humidity will create \noverbearing heat index values in the upper 90s up to the mid\n100s. The hottest day looks to be Thursday with a few locations\nin sheltered valley areas possibly reaching 100 degrees. \n\nDid cut back temperatures today and Friday, and dew points \ntoday through Friday as the blend of models has been \nconsistently running with a warm bias. Regardless, dangerous \nheat and impacts related will be felt through the weekend. Guidance\nstill has heat indices into the upper 90s and 100s Saturday and\nSunday. It is possible that the heat products that are \ncurrently slated to end at 10PM Friday night could be extended \nthrough the weekend if confidence increases. \n\nHigh temperatures start to lower some (upper 80s to lower 90s) \non Monday as our strong H500 high pressure dome breaks down and\nmoves eastward, allowing for a front to slide into the area and\nset up shop. Abundant moisture across the area however may lock\nin heat index values in the 90s for our area Monday and \nTuesday. Confidence is medium though as the high pressure center\nwill not be overhead like it has been this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nExceptionally strong dome of high pressure at H500 will keep\nconditions mostly dry today through Thursday. Stagnant air and\npartly cloudy skies will be the trademark under the influence of\nthe stalwart ridge axis. Other than the heat, morning valley \nfog is the only weather phenomenon of note for the next 24 to \n36 hours.\n\nThe protective bubble will remain overhead today and through \nsome of Friday, before it is chiseled down by a quick-moving \ndisturbance, subsequently moving eastward late Friday into \nSaturday. Chances for isolated showers and thunderstorms return\nas a result come late Friday afternoon and evening. SPC has a\nmarginal risk (level 1/5) for severe thunderstorms across our \narea Friday for isolated coverage of severe thunderstorms. The\nmain risk would be damaging downbursts due to the abundant CAPE\nthat will be entrenched across the area. Anything that forms\nwill likely be pulse oriented as deep-layer shear will be very\nweak in this environment, killing off convective growth\npotential. \n\nShower and thunderstorm probabilities and coverage gradually\nincrease through the weekend with scattered activity possible \nSaturday with the \"dirty ridge\" remaining an influence. A \npseudo-stationary front will drop south out of The Great Lakes \narea Sunday night into Monday. Chances for scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms exist Sunday afternoon\nthrough Tuesday with this front hanging around. Rounds of heavy\nrainfall will likely accompany with PWATs hovering between 1.50\"\nand 2.00\". Will have to monitor the potential for severe weather\nas instability will be high and shear will increase between\n20-40 knots with proximity to the stationary front.",
